  "query_preview": "..(Friday night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 520 AM EDT THU SEP 10 2026\n\nGeneral model agreement remains regarding the long wave pattern \nthrough the middle of next week. Amplified and progressive flow will \nmodulate across the northern half of the CONUS, while ridging \ngradually consolidates and expands west over the southeastern \nportion of the U.S. Eastern Kentucky will reside between a departing \nupper trough off of New England and a weak upper trough over the Mid-\nSouth region. At the surface, a weak cold front will be positioned \nnear the Tennessee border back towards the lower Mississippi Valley. \nEastern Kentucky will find itself in an unsettled pattern continuing \ninto early this weekend, as troughing is maintained aloft, and the \nsurface boundary lingers in the vicinity of the region. Model \nguidance has favored a slower transition to increasing 500 mb \nheights compared to yesterday. This will result in higher PoPs \nacross the area through Saturday, with some locally heavy rainers \nstill possible.\n\nSunday through Tuesday, more decisive ridging will build in aloft, \nwhich will limit the areal coverage of any convection. \nUnfortunately, this will also allow for more above normal heat and \nhumidity, with highs returning to the mid to upper 80s, and even \nsome lower 90s at times. Dew points will also likely exceed 70 \ndegrees for the majority of the period. By Wednesday, the upper \nlevel ridge may broaden and pivot enough to allow for enough passing \nshortwave energy to drive the next surface cold front south of the \nOhio River. This will bring another uptick in PoPs by Wednesday, \nalthough confidence in timing is pretty low this far out.",
